+## Verified Windows setup and changes (PSC3M6)
+
+The following records the setup completed on **2026-11-10/11**. The successful
+outcome was a built and programmed responder, working SPDM negotiation and
+certificate-fragment transfers, and an Eclipse import. **A complete
+`CHALLENGE_AUTH` run was not finished**; see the verification status below.
+
+### Build defaults and environment
+
+The application defaults in `common.mk` were changed from PSC3M5/P1 to
+`TARGET=APP_KIT_PSC3M6_EVAL` and `SPDM_PROFILE=P4`, matching the actual board
+and its ML-DSA-87 identity. No manual change to the cryptographic algorithms
+was needed for this bring-up.
+
+The working tool combination was ModusToolbox tools **3.9**, GNU Arm **14.2.1**,
+native Windows **CMake 3.31.6**, and Edge Protect Security Suite **2.4.0**.
+CMake was missing initially and was added as a portable installation. Its
+`bin` directory must be on the PATH used by both command-line builds and
+Eclipse; ModusToolbox's bundled Ninja was sufficient.
+
+The original deeply nested path contained spaces and parentheses and broke
+the Windows/Cygwin build. A temporary `subst P:` mapping to the directory
+containing the SPDM applications avoided that problem without copying files.
+Prefer a short checkout path for a new installation. `P:` is only a local
+alias and must be recreated after a Windows restart if the generated project
+paths still use it.
+
+For example, adapt the paths below to your installation. The example assumes
+that `P:` maps to the parent containing `psoc-spdm-responder`, and that native
+CMake is installed under `C:/Tools/cmake`:
+
+```powershell
+$env:CY_TOOLS_PATHS = 'C:/Infineon/Tools/ModusToolbox/tools_3.9'
+$env:HOME = $env:USERPROFILE.Replace('\', '/')
+$env:PATH = 'C:/Tools/cmake/bin;C:/Infineon/Tools/ModusToolbox/tools_3.9/modus-shell/bin;' + $env:PATH
+$make = Join-Path $env:CY_TOOLS_PATHS 'modus-shell/bin/make.exe'
+& $make -C P:/psoc-spdm-responder getlibs
+& $make -C P:/psoc-spdm-responder build TARGET=APP_KIT_PSC3M6_EVAL SPDM_PROFILE=P4
+& $make -C P:/psoc-spdm-responder eclipse TARGET=APP_KIT_PSC3M6_EVAL SPDM_PROFILE=P4 BSP_PROGRAM_INTERFACE=JLink
+```
+
+Run the environment setup again in a new shell. Changes to one shell's PATH
+do not update an already running Eclipse process. GCC and Edge Protect tools
+also need to be discoverable by the IDE build; the local Eclipse starter used
+for this setup was extended with the CMake path. This machine-specific starter
+is outside the responder repository, so configure your own launcher or IDE
+environment for a fresh checkout.
+
+### Repairing stale generated BSP files
+
+The first responder build lacked device-library metadata. Running `make
+getlibs` restored it. The Device Configurator then rejected changed/stale
+generated symbols. The existing `GeneratedSource` directory was backed up,
+and the files were regenerated from the existing `design.modus`:
+
+```powershell
+& (Join-Path $env:CY_TOOLS_PATHS 'device-configurator/device-configurator-cli.exe') --build 'P:/psoc-spdm-responder/bsps/TARGET_APP_KIT_PSC3M6_EVAL/config/design.modus' --readonly --force
+```
+
+This was a recovery step, not a required action on every build. Back up any
+manual generated-file changes before using `--force`. The source
+`design.modus` and the PSC3M6 security/lifecycle policy were not changed; the
+M6 memory partition comes from the Device Configurator, not M5 OEM-policy
+provisioning.
+
+### USB bridge firmware and mode
+
+- The original MiniProg4 was in DAPLink mode, which the Windows DFU host did
+   not expose as an I2C bridge. Switching that selected probe to `kp3-hid`
+   restored I2C enumeration. Its firmware was not updated during this test.
+- The detached CY8CPROTO KitProg3 initially ran firmware **1.01.158**. Its
+   firmware was updated, with explicit approval, to **2.90.1804** using the
+   installed `fw-loader --update-kp3` command for that probe's serial number.
+   This updates the programmer, not the PSC3 application.
+- For that updated KitProg3, **`kp3-bulk`** was the mode that actually exposed
+   I2C to `dfuh-cli`; HID did not. Do not apply a blanket HID/BULK rule across
+   different hardware and firmware versions. Check the actual enumeration with
+   `dfuh-cli --display-hw` and select the corresponding `--hwid`.
+- The MODE button is not a start button for the SPDM test. No special SPDM
+   firmware is needed on KitProg3: the standard firmware supplies the bridge.
+
+The tested I2C address was **0x08**. MiniProg4 worked at **400 kHz**; the final
+KitProg3 transfer tests used **100 kHz**. Wrong SWD-vs-I2C connections, reversed
+reference/ground wires, disconnected jumpers, USB unplugging, and a laptop
+power interruption were encountered during bring-up. Keep the data wires
+securely attached and the laptop on external power for long tests. A failed
+initial Enter-DFU write (`dfuh-cli` error 3 / exit 8195) happens **before SPDM**
+and is not evidence of a certificate or signature rejection.
+
+### Provisioning and programming the responder
+
+The separate provisioning application and host utility were used first. The
+Python host environment required **PyYAML** and **cryptography >= 46** for
+ML-DSA support; the installed cryptography version was 50.0.2. The device
+generated its own ML-DSA-87 key in **PAS slot 0**. A CSR was signed with a demo
+CA, and the resulting **15,168-byte** SPDM certificate chain was stored in
+**PAS slot 1**, exposed by the responder as **SPDM certificate slot 0**.
+Readback matched the uploaded chain byte-for-byte, and the provisioning
+`prove-cert` command verified possession of the device key.
+
+The responder was then built and programmed through the PSC3's **on-board
+J-Link**, not through the I2C-only KitProg3 wiring. The selected probe variable
+was **`MTB_PROBE_SERIAL`**, not `PROBE_SERIAL`; set it to your board's J-Link
+serial when multiple probes are connected. Programming and verification
+succeeded and retained the provisioned assets. No full-device erase,
+`--freeze`/finalize operation, or lifecycle change was performed. Do not erase
+the PAS assets when switching between provisioning and responder applications.
+
+### Eclipse export and import
+
+`make eclipse` generated all three projects:
+
+- `psoc-spdm-responder` (application)
+- `psoc-spdm-responder.proj_cm33_s` (secure)
+- `psoc-spdm-responder.proj_cm33_ns` (non-secure)
+
+Import the application **in place**, with copying into the workspace disabled.
+If Eclipse's generic existing-projects importer finds only the application,
+import `proj_cm33_s` and `proj_cm33_ns` separately as well. All three were
+imported into the existing workspace without replacing its previous projects.
+
+Eclipse initially logged workspace-lock errors while saving imported project
+preferences, and interrupted local-history backup jobs on a subsequent load.
+Regular exits/restarts after the import completed produced a clean final
+session; the workspace and project preferences were not deleted.
+
+Build and Program/Debug use the **J-Link** launch configuration. Eclipse runs
+the device firmware; the Windows **SPDM Requester remains a separate host
+program**. Opening the project in Eclipse does not automatically run
+`CHALLENGE_AUTH` or remove the DFU host's per-transfer overhead.
+
+### Verification status and remaining work
+
+| Check | Observed result |
+| ----- | --------------- |
+| P4 responder build and programming | Passed; programming verification succeeded |
+| Asset provisioning, readback and `prove-cert` | Passed; no freeze/finalize |
+| Responder startup | Certificate chain loaded, context ready, I2C listening |
+| SPDM negotiation | SPDM 1.4, ML-DSA-87, ML-KEM-1024, SHA-384, AES-256-GCM |
+| SPDM certificate transfer | Successful fragments observed; full SPDM certificate validation not completed |
+| Eclipse export and import | All three projects imported; clean final restarted session |
+| `CHALLENGE_AUTH`, signed measurements, secure session and encrypted exchange | Not verified end-to-end |
+
+The final authentication run was deliberately cancelled before completion to
+continue with the Eclipse import. Earlier runs were also interrupted by
+physical disconnection or power loss. A manually cancelled run reporting
+`SPDM_REQ_EAUTH` is **not** proof of an invalid signature. Provisioning
+`prove-cert` is a different check from SPDM `CHALLENGE_AUTH`.
+
+Measured Windows host/bridge initialization took about **11 seconds per DFU
+transaction**, while a small actual GET_VERSION exchange completed in under
+one second after initialization. Certificate and ML-DSA-87 signature responses
+are fragmented into small packets, so a full run can take many minutes.
+Allow it to finish uninterrupted before claiming authenticated SPDM success.
+
+For GitHub, publish portable sources, BSP templates, dependency definitions
+and the libspdm submodule; exclude build/IDE caches, local test artefacts and
+private keys. Do not rely on a developer's personal paths, `P:` mapping or
+external Eclipse starter. The provisioning applications and host tools are
+separate from this responder repository and must be obtained separately.
+
+
